1
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:00,890
Good morning, Quark.

2
00:03:03,517 --> 00:03:05,519
Couldn't believe it.
Lisa told Janet

3
00:03:05,644 --> 00:03:08,314
that Paul asked Barbara to ask me
if I had a date to the dance.

4
00:03:09,231 --> 00:03:11,859
No. They broke up
for religious differences.

5
00:03:11,942 --> 00:03:14,069
She thought she was God
and he disagreed.

6
00:03:16,405 --> 00:03:18,449
Yeah, this afternoon
around five.

7
00:03:19,158 --> 00:03:20,910
I'm meeting him at the mall.

8
00:03:20,993 --> 00:03:23,621
I don't know. Maybe. Yeah.

9
00:03:25,664 --> 00:03:26,624
Oh, I can't.

10
00:03:26,707 --> 00:03:29,001
I've got to hang around awhile
and keep an eye on Nick.

11
00:03:29,543 --> 00:03:31,128
He likes to take things apart.

12
00:03:32,630 --> 00:03:34,965
Well, that's why I watch it
at your house.

13
00:03:35,674 --> 00:03:37,593
Things will cool off after today.

14
00:03:37,968 --> 00:03:39,887
Dad has his big "show and tell"
at the lab.

15
00:03:41,388 --> 00:03:42,389
Zap!

16
00:03:43,891 --> 00:03:47,561
- It works! It really works!
- Nick, get real.

17
00:03:48,854 --> 00:03:50,940
The subject... has disappeared.

18
00:03:51,065 --> 00:03:52,733
No, no, it's not that.

19
00:03:53,525 --> 00:03:54,777
No.

20
00:03:55,527 --> 00:03:57,571
Mom and Dad
had an argument last night,

21
00:03:57,655 --> 00:03:59,281
and Mom spent the night at Grandma's.

22
00:03:59,698 --> 00:04:01,325
I think she needed a rest.

23
00:04:01,659 --> 00:04:03,369
Right.
Her and me both.

24
00:04:03,786 --> 00:04:04,954
Yeah, okay.

25
00:04:05,663 --> 00:04:07,248
Okay. Bye.

26
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:15,756
Amy... when's Mom coming home?

27
00:04:16,298 --> 00:04:19,301
She's working, Nick.
She'll be home this afternoon.

28
00:04:22,638 --> 00:04:24,223
If only Dad could get
that machine to work.

29
00:04:47,621 --> 00:04:48,789
Breakfast.

30
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,668
I need a couple of minutes here.

31
00:04:56,922 --> 00:05:00,301
"I'll be down in five minutes."

32
00:05:01,927 --> 00:05:03,804
Typing with his gloves on again.

33
00:05:32,624 --> 00:05:35,085
Oh, no, not again!

34
00:05:35,544 --> 00:05:37,838
Who does he think he is,
Mister Wizard?

35
00:05:40,799 --> 00:05:43,886
Szalinski, give it a rest!
It's Saturday!

36
00:05:45,679 --> 00:05:47,306
It's construction, honey.

37
00:05:47,389 --> 00:05:49,475
You of all people
should understand that.

38
00:05:49,641 --> 00:05:50,058
- You're much bigger than he is.
- Szalinski!

39
00:05:51,977 --> 00:05:53,562
- Honey, can't we--
- Hold it, Dad.

40
00:05:54,646 --> 00:05:55,439
Don't move.

41
00:05:55,522 --> 00:05:57,941
- Ron, what are you doing?
- Defending the backyard, Dad.

42
00:05:58,066 --> 00:05:59,276
That's my fishing rod!

43
00:05:59,359 --> 00:06:00,235
Dad, no!

44
00:06:00,318 --> 00:06:02,571
Dad!

45
00:06:05,323 --> 00:06:06,283
Am I bleeding?

46
00:06:06,408 --> 00:06:08,201
Honey, it's rubber.

47
00:06:08,285 --> 00:06:11,037
- Dad, I told you not to move.
- What's on this thing?

48
00:06:11,663 --> 00:06:14,040
- Super Glue.
- Super Glue?

49
00:06:14,291 --> 00:06:15,959
Gotta mark those trespassers, Dad.

50
00:06:16,084 --> 00:06:17,586
I'm not a trespasser.
I'm your father!

51
00:06:17,669 --> 00:06:19,129
Better wash it off quick, hon.

52
00:06:19,379 --> 00:06:21,548
- It's taking the skin off.
- I'll talk to you later.

53
00:06:21,715 --> 00:06:24,134
- What's with this kid?
- He's 12.

54
00:06:24,301 --> 00:06:25,969
Attila the Hun
was 12 once, too, you know.

55
00:06:26,052 --> 00:06:27,262
Oh, come on!

56
00:06:29,097 --> 00:06:30,307
He's sounding.

57
00:06:33,769 --> 00:06:35,020
Weird family.

58
00:06:50,076 --> 00:06:51,161
Dad?

59
00:06:51,953 --> 00:06:53,663
Look, I finished mine.

60
00:06:53,997 --> 00:06:56,333
- Looks just like yours, huh?
- That's great, Nick.

61
00:06:57,459 --> 00:06:59,336
You know, Dad, I was calculating.

62
00:06:59,586 --> 00:07:00,712
If you took all the molecules
in this house--

63
00:07:00,796 --> 00:07:03,089
Listen, Nick, I gotta get this thing
working before I leave.

64
00:07:03,173 --> 00:07:04,508
Why don't you help your sister?

65
00:07:48,176 --> 00:07:49,302
Stay back, boy.

66
00:07:49,761 --> 00:07:50,762
If this thing works,

67
00:07:50,846 --> 00:07:53,431
this'll put us right up there
with the invention of electricity,

68
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:56,309
the first man in space.

69
00:07:56,393 --> 00:07:57,644
First dog in space.

70
00:08:05,652 --> 00:08:08,154
Come on! Shrink!

71
00:08:08,905 --> 00:08:09,906
Shrink!

72
00:08:18,290 --> 00:08:20,292
Please, please, shrink.

73
00:08:20,792 --> 00:08:21,835
Shrink!

74
00:08:36,641 --> 00:08:37,434
On the other hand,

75
00:08:37,517 --> 00:08:40,145
we've come up with an interesting way
of making applesauce.

76
00:08:45,483 --> 00:08:46,610
You got it, honey.

77
00:08:46,693 --> 00:08:48,236
- Sure?
- Positive.

78
00:08:48,361 --> 00:08:51,406
You're wiping away brain cells,
and there aren't many to spare.

79
00:08:52,532 --> 00:08:54,492
Everybody in my family's a comedian.

80
00:08:54,618 --> 00:08:56,328
Everybody except you, hon.

81
00:08:57,621 --> 00:08:58,955
Darn right.

82
00:09:01,499 --> 00:09:03,960
Well, look who's decided
to join the land of the living.

83
00:09:04,753 --> 00:09:06,713
And is Russell ready
for his big fishing trip?

84
00:09:07,547 --> 00:09:08,715
Not really, Dad.

85
00:09:09,382 --> 00:09:10,467
Not really?

86
00:09:10,550 --> 00:09:12,510
There's a big fish out there
with your name on it.

87
00:09:13,762 --> 00:09:15,931
Fishing's your thing,
Dad, not mine.

88
00:09:18,892 --> 00:09:20,852
- What's the matter with him?
- Shh!

89
00:09:20,936 --> 00:09:22,103
What do you mean "shush"?

90
00:09:22,228 --> 00:09:24,940
I see nothing wrong
with wanting to take my son fishing.

91
00:09:25,857 --> 00:09:27,525
Russel, I shouldn't
be telling you this,

92
00:09:27,609 --> 00:09:29,486
but he was cut
from the football team yesterday.

93
00:09:29,569 --> 00:09:30,987
- What?
- Honey, please!

94
00:09:31,071 --> 00:09:32,864
What he doesn't need right now
is to hear from you.

95
00:09:32,948 --> 00:09:33,865
He needs something.

96
00:09:33,949 --> 00:09:35,784
- When I was his age, I was cap--
- Captain of the team.

97
00:09:35,867 --> 00:09:37,661
And if my father wanted me
to go on a fishing trip,

98
00:09:37,744 --> 00:09:38,870
- I would've been--
- Been grateful.

99
00:09:38,954 --> 00:09:40,956
Russ, he is too small for football.

100
00:09:41,039 --> 00:09:43,416
- He is not too small for football!
- Too small!

101
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:45,710
Shh! Honey, when you say
things like that,

102
00:09:45,794 --> 00:09:47,045
you make him feel about this big.

103
00:09:47,128 --> 00:09:50,048
If he wants to feel big,
he should act big.

104
00:09:55,303 --> 00:09:56,596
Elbows up.

105
00:10:00,558 --> 00:10:01,768
What's the matter with you?

106
00:10:01,851 --> 00:10:03,061
I am on a special diet.

107
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:05,313
- No toxic waste.
- Fine.

108
00:10:07,941 --> 00:10:09,275
Here, Quark.

109
00:10:13,571 --> 00:10:15,490
Eat it, Dad, before it gets cold.

110
00:10:21,496 --> 00:10:23,164
Mmm, did you get the machine to work?

111
00:10:24,332 --> 00:10:25,375
Yeah.

112
00:10:25,583 --> 00:10:27,168
A few more bugs to get out, but...

113
00:10:27,961 --> 00:10:29,129
What about the conference?

114
00:10:31,923 --> 00:10:33,508
Jeez, the conference.
I gotta get going.

115
00:10:35,218 --> 00:10:37,554
Dad! Dad, wait.

116
00:10:37,887 --> 00:10:38,972
Dad!

117
00:10:39,055 --> 00:10:41,349
If Mom calls, tell her
I'll be home by lunchtime.

118
00:10:41,433 --> 00:10:43,268
Dad, don't forget my dress
from the cleaners,

119
00:10:43,351 --> 00:10:45,687
- Nick's allergy pills--
- It's all in my head. Don't worry.

120
00:10:46,146 --> 00:10:47,230
Wish me luck!

121
00:10:47,772 --> 00:10:49,357
I need luck getting out
of this door.

122
00:10:56,072 --> 00:10:57,365
House of Insanity.

123
00:10:58,158 --> 00:10:59,909
- Hi, honey.
<i>- Hi, Mom.</i>

124
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:01,244
Nick, go get Dad.

125
00:11:01,327 --> 00:11:03,246
He's just leaving for the conference.

126
00:11:03,329 --> 00:11:05,623
Dad, it's Mom.

127
00:11:06,541 --> 00:11:07,876
- Where?
- On the phone!

128
00:11:08,501 --> 00:11:09,252
Oh.

129
00:11:09,335 --> 00:11:10,754
How did everything go this morning?

130
00:11:10,879 --> 00:11:12,756
Boy, did we miss you.

131
00:11:13,006 --> 00:11:14,340
I missed you too, honey.

132
00:11:14,799 --> 00:11:16,301
Did Paul Tate ask you to the dance?

133
00:11:16,384 --> 00:11:17,927
I think he's going to.

134
00:11:18,011 --> 00:11:19,721
I'm gonna meet him
at the mall later today.

135
00:11:20,096 --> 00:11:21,514
- Here's Dad.
- Thanks.

136
00:11:22,223 --> 00:11:23,433
- Hiya.
<i>- Hi.</i>

137
00:11:23,516 --> 00:11:24,309
Nick!

138
00:11:24,434 --> 00:11:26,019
I called to wish you
the luck at the conference.

139
00:11:26,269 --> 00:11:27,312
Look, Diane,

140
00:11:27,771 --> 00:11:28,897
if things go well this morning,

141
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:30,899
everything will change around here,
I promise you.

142
00:11:31,149 --> 00:11:33,401
<i>Okay. I have a house to show,</i>

143
00:11:34,110 --> 00:11:36,196
and then I'll be home this afternoon
and we'll talk.

144
00:11:36,279 --> 00:11:37,280
<i>Good luck today.</i>

145
00:11:37,405 --> 00:11:39,365
Thanks. Bye, honey.

146
00:11:40,492 --> 00:11:42,535
- What time is Mom coming home?
- Later.

147
00:11:42,786 --> 00:11:44,621
Listen, we got to make this place
spick and span. Okay?

148
00:11:44,704 --> 00:11:47,082
Nick, I want you to mow the lawn.
Amy...

149
00:11:50,752 --> 00:11:51,795
Good luck.

150
00:12:03,098 --> 00:12:06,351
I'm helping my dad with this
real important new invention.

151
00:12:06,684 --> 00:12:08,478
So, um...

152
00:12:09,562 --> 00:12:11,606
I... I'll let you mow half my yard.

153
00:12:12,524 --> 00:12:13,900
No, thanks.

154
00:12:16,236 --> 00:12:18,029
Wow! With that?

155
00:12:18,363 --> 00:12:19,364
Yeah!

156
00:12:20,198 --> 00:12:22,617
And... this.

157
00:12:25,912 --> 00:12:27,956
Oh, it's a remote control!

158
00:12:28,665 --> 00:12:31,334
Tell you what.

159
00:12:31,584 --> 00:12:35,380
You throw in... a box of cookies,
I'll let you cut the whole thing.

160
00:12:36,589 --> 00:12:39,425
Oh, but I have to go
and meet Newt right now.

161
00:12:41,636 --> 00:12:43,012
Uh, could I do it later?

162
00:12:44,055 --> 00:12:45,306
I don't think so,
because, you know,

163
00:12:45,390 --> 00:12:47,183
- my dad's gonna come home and...
- Aw, come on!

164
00:12:48,810 --> 00:12:50,353
Okay. Don't be too late.

165
00:12:50,687 --> 00:12:51,896
Okay, thanks.

166
00:12:52,272 --> 00:12:53,565
No problem.

167
00:12:53,857 --> 00:12:55,108
See you later!

168
00:13:01,114 --> 00:13:03,992
Nothing like a hard day's work.

169
00:13:08,163 --> 00:13:09,581
French fries,

170
00:13:11,624 --> 00:13:12,917
Tater sticks,

171
00:13:14,627 --> 00:13:15,795
meat loaf,

172
00:13:17,005 --> 00:13:18,882
veal "parmedjian."

173
00:13:19,215 --> 00:13:20,884
Hey, Dad.
Wanna go play some baseball?

174
00:13:21,426 --> 00:13:23,136
Hmm, baseball...

175
00:13:24,554 --> 00:13:25,597
No.

176
00:13:25,972 --> 00:13:27,724
I gotta load the camper, Ron.
I'm busy.

177
00:13:28,850 --> 00:13:30,185
Hey, Russ, pitch for me.

178
00:13:48,411 --> 00:13:49,787
Hey, Szalinski,

179
00:13:50,371 --> 00:13:52,248
ever do anything normal,
like play baseball?

180
00:13:52,457 --> 00:13:53,541
Nope.

181
00:13:54,792 --> 00:13:56,419
Baseball's for mortals.

182
00:13:56,628 --> 00:13:58,004
Maybe you could be the base.

183
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:00,924
Maybe you could be the mound.

184
00:14:01,299 --> 00:14:02,383
Ronald!

185
00:14:02,884 --> 00:14:04,010
Lug your stuff.

186
00:14:04,844 --> 00:14:06,179
Later, worm.

187
00:14:13,519 --> 00:14:15,104
Russell, give me a hand here.

188
00:14:18,691 --> 00:14:21,277
Russell! Give me a hand here.

189
00:14:23,112 --> 00:14:25,198
Dad, it's early.

190
00:14:26,199 --> 00:14:28,868
The early worm catches the fish,
Russell.

191
00:14:30,453 --> 00:14:31,496
Remember that.

192
00:14:33,289 --> 00:14:35,917
The Forresters are coming by
after lunch, so come on! Quick march!

193
00:15:09,951 --> 00:15:11,327
Weird family.

194
00:15:32,557 --> 00:15:33,641
Russell...

195
00:15:35,852 --> 00:15:39,230
I know it seems strange
when you look at me today, but,

196
00:15:39,647 --> 00:15:40,857
when I was your age,

197
00:15:41,149 --> 00:15:43,067
I was not much bigger than you.

198
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:45,820
I know, Dad. You've told me
about a million times.

199
00:15:45,903 --> 00:15:47,447
All right, just hear me out.

200
00:15:47,655 --> 00:15:50,491
The point is,
coach put me on these babies.

201
00:15:50,992 --> 00:15:52,118
Know what happened?

202
00:15:52,785 --> 00:15:55,538
I put on 20 pounds
of pure, unadulterated,

203
00:15:55,621 --> 00:15:57,874
blitz-the-quarterback and
rip-his-head-off muscle, Russell.

204
00:15:58,833 --> 00:16:00,752
Now, I'll make you a little bet.

205
00:16:01,252 --> 00:16:04,088
If you work out for... three months,

206
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:07,592
you will be throwing farther,
you will be running faster,

207
00:16:07,759 --> 00:16:10,720
and you will be hitting harder
than anybody else on that team.

208
00:16:35,495 --> 00:16:36,704
Go ahead, go ahead.

209
00:16:38,831 --> 00:16:42,335
Um, Dad, you know
the weights aren't my thing.

210
00:16:44,087 --> 00:16:45,630
Fishing isn't your thing.

211
00:16:46,214 --> 00:16:49,592
Weights aren't your thing.
Just what is your thing?

212
00:16:52,178 --> 00:16:53,429
I don't know,

213
00:16:53,805 --> 00:16:55,515
but I'll keep you posted, okay?

214
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:59,268
Fine, until you do,
you're trying these,

215
00:16:59,936 --> 00:17:02,605
and you're coming fishing
with the family. Is that clear?

216
00:17:03,856 --> 00:17:05,900
I can't hear you!

217
00:17:06,776 --> 00:17:08,027
Yes, sir.

218
00:17:09,237 --> 00:17:10,446
Better.

219
00:17:11,697 --> 00:17:12,907
Uh...

220
00:17:13,533 --> 00:17:15,493
lift with your legs,
not with your back.

221
00:17:30,049 --> 00:17:31,884
What am I doing wrong, Spike?

222
00:17:40,435 --> 00:17:42,812
Russell, you're not smoking
a cigarette, are you?

223
00:17:42,895 --> 00:17:44,981
No, dear.
You know I've given that up.

224
00:17:59,162 --> 00:18:01,247
- Your stuff all loaded?
- Ready to fire.

225
00:18:01,664 --> 00:18:03,499
We've got the best spot on the lake.
You looking forward to it?

226
00:18:03,583 --> 00:18:04,625
Tubular!

227
00:18:06,419 --> 00:18:08,963
He's only 12 and he's
already thinking about construction.

228
00:18:10,298 --> 00:18:12,008
Why can't Russell be more like that?

229
00:18:12,091 --> 00:18:14,093
Oh, honey, just give him a chance.

230
00:18:22,185 --> 00:18:23,603
Ron Thompson at the plate.

231
00:18:23,686 --> 00:18:25,730
Bottom of the 9th. Clutch situation.

232
00:18:26,022 --> 00:18:27,356
Here comes the pitch!

233
00:18:27,899 --> 00:18:30,943
Oh, he's swinging like a rusty gate!

234
00:18:31,486 --> 00:18:33,613
He pumps once, pumps twice. Oh!

235
00:18:34,030 --> 00:18:36,491
He's never been good
on the high outside pitch.

236
00:18:36,782 --> 00:18:39,285
Here it comes.
The fastball is bread and butter.

237
00:18:39,911 --> 00:18:40,912
Grand...

238
00:18:43,998 --> 00:18:45,041
slam.

239
00:18:49,462 --> 00:18:51,172
Hey, hey, I didn't do it.

240
00:18:51,506 --> 00:18:53,007
- Come on, dope.
- Nobody has to know.

241
00:18:53,090 --> 00:18:54,258
Nobody saw it but you!

242
00:18:55,009 --> 00:18:58,137
Let's negotiate.
I'm your brother, Russ.

243
00:18:58,304 --> 00:19:00,139
You're not supposed
to fink on your own bother!

244
00:19:01,641 --> 00:19:04,393
Turn me in, and I'll tell all what
you spend your allowance on!

245
00:19:04,477 --> 00:19:05,770
Come on, Russ.

246
00:19:07,730 --> 00:19:10,525
Look, either you tell 'em,
or I tell 'em, okay?

247
00:19:12,193 --> 00:19:14,111
Okay, you tell 'em.

248
00:19:30,753 --> 00:19:32,255
Aren't you in the wrong yard?

249
00:19:32,463 --> 00:19:34,006
- Listen, nerd face...
- Tell 'em!

250
00:19:34,382 --> 00:19:35,466
What's going on?

251
00:19:36,008 --> 00:19:37,843
Hi. Um...

252
00:19:38,886 --> 00:19:40,846
I'm Russ Thompson from next door.

253
00:19:41,889 --> 00:19:42,848
Um...

254
00:19:43,558 --> 00:19:45,351
Um, my brother has
something to tell you.

255
00:19:45,851 --> 00:19:47,019
Or else I can tell her.

256
00:19:47,186 --> 00:19:48,271
Okay.

257
00:19:48,729 --> 00:19:50,356
See, I was playing
with my ball, right?

258
00:19:50,481 --> 00:19:53,484
Well, actually, it's not my ball
exactly. It's Charlie Sudzen's...

259
00:19:53,568 --> 00:19:54,569
Just tell her what you did.

260
00:19:54,777 --> 00:19:57,238
It never would have happened
if their house wasn't so close.

261
00:19:58,114 --> 00:19:59,782
He hit a baseball
through your attic window.

262
00:19:59,907 --> 00:20:00,825
He what?

263
00:20:00,908 --> 00:20:03,911
It shouldn't have been closed
in the first place. It's a nice day!

264
00:20:03,995 --> 00:20:06,038
We'll get it fixed, okay? We'll take
it out of his allowance, all right?

265
00:20:06,122 --> 00:20:07,748
My allowance? Dream on.

266
00:20:07,832 --> 00:20:09,709
Okay. We'll just have
Dad pay for it, huh?

267
00:20:10,501 --> 00:20:12,753
We'll take it out of my allowance.
Give me my ball back.

268
00:20:12,837 --> 00:20:15,631
Until you pay, no man shall pass.

269
00:20:15,715 --> 00:20:17,675
You've got nothing to say about it,
space boy.

270
00:20:17,758 --> 00:20:18,884
Cool it!

271
00:20:20,136 --> 00:20:22,430
Nick, take him upstairs
and get him his ball.

272
00:20:22,513 --> 00:20:23,973
- And clean up the mess.
- What?

273
00:20:24,056 --> 00:20:26,392
Do it, Nick.
I don't have time to mess around.

274
00:20:28,603 --> 00:20:29,437
Come on.

275
00:20:29,562 --> 00:20:32,398
This is what's wrong
with the American system of justice.

276
00:20:33,941 --> 00:20:34,984
So...

277
00:20:36,569 --> 00:20:37,945
You like to dance, huh?

278
00:20:39,280 --> 00:20:40,489
How do you know?

279
00:20:42,283 --> 00:20:43,034
Well...

280
00:20:43,117 --> 00:20:44,827
- You could see me?
- No!

281
00:20:44,910 --> 00:20:46,621
No... Yeah, I could...

282
00:20:47,622 --> 00:20:49,040
but I wasn't watching you, I...

283
00:20:53,002 --> 00:20:54,170
Awesome.

284
00:20:54,754 --> 00:20:57,256
So your dad's still in contact
with his home planet.

285
00:21:09,101 --> 00:21:11,896
And given that my machine
can substantially reduce the size

286
00:21:11,979 --> 00:21:13,981
of bulky payloads...

287
00:21:15,358 --> 00:21:16,859
and fuel supplies,

288
00:21:17,151 --> 00:21:19,612
the savings to the space program
would be staggering.

289
00:21:19,904 --> 00:21:21,155
Mr. Szalinski,

290
00:21:21,405 --> 00:21:24,367
are you trying to tell me that
suddenly size is no longer relative?

291
00:21:24,659 --> 00:21:26,410
Well, that's right,
Professor Fredrickson,

292
00:21:26,494 --> 00:21:29,372
that all matter is made up of not
only density, but of empty space.

293
00:21:29,455 --> 00:21:31,082
And if we can proportionately reduce

294
00:21:31,165 --> 00:21:32,875
the amount of empty space
in any given object,

295
00:21:32,958 --> 00:21:34,835
we can thereby... shrink the object.

296
00:21:34,919 --> 00:21:36,504
Uh-huh. Where's your proof?

297
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:40,007
When Einstein came up
with the atomic bomb,

298
00:21:40,132 --> 00:21:41,676
did they ask him to prove
that it worked?

299
00:21:42,176 --> 00:21:44,887
You, Mr. Szalinski,
are hardly Einstein.

300
00:21:47,223 --> 00:21:48,349
I picked a name.

301
00:21:48,474 --> 00:21:50,643
You have, however, managed
to shrink one thing.

302
00:21:50,976 --> 00:21:52,687
The size of this audience.

303
00:21:52,978 --> 00:21:55,022
Gentlemen, ladies,
I don't know about you,

304
00:21:55,106 --> 00:21:56,565
but I'm going to lunch.

305
00:22:04,490 --> 00:22:06,325
Wayne? Wayne!

306
00:22:07,284 --> 00:22:10,371
- Oh, hi, Dr. Brainard.
- Don't take it too hard, Wayne.

307
00:22:10,663 --> 00:22:13,332
It will take time
to convince people without proof.

308
00:22:14,125 --> 00:22:16,001
I appreciate it.
Thanks a lot.

309
00:22:17,795 --> 00:22:20,715
By the way, you were right
about the electric flea collar.

310
00:22:20,798 --> 00:22:22,633
The extension cord was a bad idea.

311
00:22:23,592 --> 00:22:25,261
What could be taking them so long?

312
00:22:26,887 --> 00:22:28,848
I am not missing the mall today.

313
00:22:30,015 --> 00:22:31,183
Nick!

314
00:22:34,562 --> 00:22:35,813
- Nick.
- Ron?

315
00:22:35,896 --> 00:22:38,065
If you guys are hiding up here,
it's not funny.

316
00:22:39,150 --> 00:22:40,818
Nick, you know what Dad said.

317
00:22:41,277 --> 00:22:43,195
You're not supposed
to be playing up here.

318
00:22:58,794 --> 00:23:00,045
Where are we?

319
00:23:13,017 --> 00:23:14,226
Oh, my God. What happened?

320
00:23:14,435 --> 00:23:16,020
- Amy!
- Russ!

321
00:23:16,562 --> 00:23:18,022
Amy!

322
00:23:19,648 --> 00:23:20,649
Nicky.

323
00:23:20,733 --> 00:23:21,901
Ron?

324
00:23:24,945 --> 00:23:26,322
Nick, what happened?

325
00:23:26,405 --> 00:23:28,032
We're all the size of boogers!

326
00:23:31,827 --> 00:23:33,204
Be quiet, Quark!

327
00:23:36,123 --> 00:23:37,333
Nick, what did you do?

328
00:23:37,416 --> 00:23:39,918
- Me? It was his ball.
- Shut up, wimp!

329
00:23:39,918 --> 00:23:40,127
Can somebody get that?

330
00:23:40,211 --> 00:23:42,338
- What's that?
- It's Dad's thinking couch.

331
00:23:48,677 --> 00:23:50,513
Amy? Nick?

332
00:23:50,763 --> 00:23:51,972
Hello?

333
00:23:56,477 --> 00:23:57,686
It's not for you.

334
00:24:00,689 --> 00:24:02,107
What is it, Quark?

335
00:24:02,191 --> 00:24:04,068
Nick!

336
00:24:05,903 --> 00:24:07,947
- Listen.
- Are you guys home?

337
00:24:08,239 --> 00:24:10,282
Sounds like Dad. He'll fix us.

338
00:24:10,407 --> 00:24:12,952
Mr. Szalinski, up here!

339
00:24:13,202 --> 00:24:14,620
Up here, Dad!

340
00:24:14,787 --> 00:24:16,830
Quick, over there by the door,
okay? Get that side!

341
00:24:17,164 --> 00:24:18,332
Is anybody up there?

342
00:24:18,415 --> 00:24:20,501
Nick, get off and give us a hand!

343
00:24:22,461 --> 00:24:24,213
Nick? Amy?

344
00:24:24,755 --> 00:24:26,715
Hey, Amy, your date's here.

345
00:24:27,299 --> 00:24:28,592
Oh, gross.

346
00:24:29,009 --> 00:24:30,511
Watch out for the crack!

347
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:37,685
Hurry up. He's coming!

348
00:24:51,907 --> 00:24:54,994
Right here, Mr. Szalinski.

349
00:24:56,287 --> 00:24:57,705
- Dad!
- Dad!

350
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:09,508
Help, help!

351
00:25:17,433 --> 00:25:18,726
Where's my couch?

352
00:25:24,523 --> 00:25:27,401
- What is he, deaf?
- Don't you get it?

353
00:25:27,860 --> 00:25:29,945
We're too small. He can't hear us.

354
00:25:35,284 --> 00:25:36,410
Dad!

355
00:25:41,122 --> 00:25:43,416
- Move over!
- Ow! Watch out!

356
00:25:57,973 --> 00:25:59,349
Oh, my God.

357
00:26:01,977 --> 00:26:04,229
Reminds me of the backyard.

358
00:26:06,064 --> 00:26:08,483
Something tells me
we're not going fishing this weekend.

359
00:26:08,567 --> 00:26:10,360
That should make you happy.

360
00:26:10,443 --> 00:26:12,112
Blow it
out your shorts, Ron.

361
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:13,822
Dad can fix us, right Nick?

362
00:26:14,447 --> 00:26:17,033
I... I think so.

363
00:26:17,576 --> 00:26:19,035
RON; Oh, great.

364
00:26:19,578 --> 00:26:20,954
We just have to get back
to the house.

365
00:26:21,037 --> 00:26:22,998
I'm never going back to your house!

366
00:26:23,623 --> 00:26:24,833
I'm going home.

367
00:26:25,917 --> 00:26:27,210
Come on, Russ.

368
00:26:30,964 --> 00:26:33,216
- Russ?
- Tell me the truth.

369
00:26:33,925 --> 00:26:35,051
Can your dad help us?

370
00:26:35,260 --> 00:26:37,637
Russ, we're supposed
to go fishing.

371
00:26:37,762 --> 00:26:39,806
Yeah, right. How're you going,
as bait?

372
00:26:41,683 --> 00:26:42,976
What a witch!

373
00:26:56,156 --> 00:26:58,491
Russ, you coming down or what?

374
00:26:59,075 --> 00:27:00,577
Well, we can't stay here.

375
00:27:02,996 --> 00:27:04,831
Well, I think we should
stick together.

376
00:27:07,083 --> 00:27:08,335
Well, come on.

377
00:27:14,341 --> 00:27:15,425
Nicky!

378
00:27:37,197 --> 00:27:39,366
I don't think we're
in Kansas anymore, Toto.

379
00:27:40,659 --> 00:27:43,119
I don't think we're
in the food chain anymore, Dorothy.

380
00:27:50,126 --> 00:27:51,002
Come on, Ron.

381
00:27:51,086 --> 00:27:52,754
- We're gonna go with them.
- What?

382
00:27:53,588 --> 00:27:55,799
You let a girl tell
you what to do? Mmm.

383
00:27:56,841 --> 00:27:59,052
Look, Ron. Down here,
you're gonna listen to me.

384
00:27:59,386 --> 00:28:01,513
From now on, you're gonna do
what I say!

385
00:28:02,389 --> 00:28:03,431
Run!

386
00:28:20,407 --> 00:28:23,493
Great Sir Russell Thompson,
stalking the rogue butterfly.

387
00:28:23,618 --> 00:28:24,953
Knock it off, Ron.

388
00:28:26,246 --> 00:28:29,499
We're now a quarter
of an inch tall, and...

389
00:28:30,041 --> 00:28:32,002
64 feet from the house.

390
00:28:32,627 --> 00:28:35,505
That's the equivalent of 3.2 miles.

391
00:28:36,089 --> 00:28:39,009
That's a long way,
even for a man of science.

392
00:28:39,175 --> 00:28:41,511
Nick, I've got six hours
to get home,

393
00:28:41,594 --> 00:28:43,305
get big, and get to the mall.

394
00:28:44,222 --> 00:28:45,515
Now get moving!

395
00:28:48,184 --> 00:28:52,355
That butterfly
had a wingspan of 42 feet!

396
00:28:52,564 --> 00:28:53,898
Nick!

397
00:29:08,621 --> 00:29:09,622
Hi.

398
00:29:10,457 --> 00:29:11,708
Hi.

399
00:29:13,084 --> 00:29:14,294
- Look, Diane...
- Guess what I saw...

400
00:29:15,795 --> 00:29:17,839
- I sold a house.
- Really? That's great!

401
00:29:17,922 --> 00:29:21,051
Yeah, you know that big estate?
The Boorsteins bought it.

402
00:29:21,134 --> 00:29:23,386
I only had to show it
to them 12 times.

403
00:29:23,887 --> 00:29:25,305
How'd your conference go?

404
00:29:26,056 --> 00:29:27,432
I got a lot of laughs.

405
00:29:27,932 --> 00:29:30,602
- Oh, Wayne.
- I failed, Diane. I'm...

406
00:29:31,186 --> 00:29:34,356
I'm just gonna get my old job back,
if I can get my old job back.

407
00:29:34,606 --> 00:29:36,983
But from now on,
things are gonna be different.

408
00:29:37,275 --> 00:29:38,568
Different.

409
00:29:40,111 --> 00:29:41,196
Right.

410
00:29:44,449 --> 00:29:45,575
Where are the kids?

411
00:29:45,867 --> 00:29:48,119
I haven't seen them
since I left this morning.

412
00:29:48,536 --> 00:29:49,871
Did you tell them
I was coming home?

413
00:29:49,954 --> 00:29:51,623
Yeah, that's why
they cleaned things up.

414
00:29:52,457 --> 00:29:53,750
I see.

415
00:29:54,292 --> 00:29:55,960
Well, they're probably
just at the mall.

416
00:29:59,005 --> 00:30:01,966
Want to hear something strange?
My thinking couch is missing.

417
00:30:02,717 --> 00:30:05,261
- The one from the attic?
- Yeah.

418
00:30:14,020 --> 00:30:17,357
Hey, Ron. This is a lot better than
those nature hikes Dad drags...

419
00:30:19,359 --> 00:30:22,821
Nick, don't you ever
pick up your toys?

420
00:30:22,904 --> 00:30:24,114
What a wimp!

421
00:30:24,656 --> 00:30:26,533
Been looking for that guy.

422
00:30:30,328 --> 00:30:31,830
It's already one o'clock.

423
00:30:32,455 --> 00:30:33,665
So much for the mall.

424
00:30:34,040 --> 00:30:35,667
What if time shrunk, too?
I mean,

425
00:30:35,750 --> 00:30:39,337
what if it's hours to us, but it's
years to the rest of the world?

426
00:30:39,754 --> 00:30:42,257
Okay, that would be cool.
All my teachers would be retired.

427
00:30:42,465 --> 00:30:45,135
Impossible. Time doesn't exist,
except as a...

428
00:30:45,260 --> 00:30:46,511
No...

429
00:30:46,845 --> 00:30:48,555
What's a river doing
in your yard?

430
00:30:48,847 --> 00:30:50,348
It's not a river, dope.

431
00:30:50,432 --> 00:30:52,976
It could be a stream of dog pee
and it looks like a river to us.

432
00:30:55,103 --> 00:30:56,354
Let's swim it.

433
00:31:01,192 --> 00:31:04,154
- Have a ball, baby.
- I'm not swimming in that.

434
00:31:06,197 --> 00:31:09,993
Hey, if we could get some rope,
we could make a log bridge!

435
00:31:12,579 --> 00:31:14,414
If we... If we had some logs.

436
00:31:19,085 --> 00:31:20,128
Quark!

437
00:31:20,211 --> 00:31:21,588
- Your dog?
- Yeah.

438
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:23,131
Quark can't hear us.

439
00:31:23,214 --> 00:31:25,508
Yeah, he can. Dogs have great ears.

440
00:31:25,675 --> 00:31:28,470
If we can get on him, he'll take us
all the way back to the house.

441
00:31:28,803 --> 00:31:29,846
Whistle.

442
00:31:43,735 --> 00:31:45,987
- What's wrong?
- He can't whistle.

443
00:31:49,824 --> 00:31:51,618
Let's get higher up.

444
00:31:55,121 --> 00:31:56,539
You know how to whistle.

445
00:31:56,789 --> 00:31:59,042
You just put your lips together
and blow.

446
00:32:09,010 --> 00:32:11,930
From the top of that flower,
we could see over the grass.

447
00:32:13,973 --> 00:32:15,308
Let's climb!

448
00:32:21,105 --> 00:32:23,900
No, Patty, no, no.
There's nothing to worry about.

449
00:32:24,108 --> 00:32:26,569
Just have Amy call me
if you hear from her. Thanks.

450
00:32:28,321 --> 00:32:30,323
Amy's not at Patty's, and Nick's not
at the Koestlers'.

451
00:32:30,406 --> 00:32:32,200
I'm gonna call Betsy.

452
00:32:32,408 --> 00:32:34,661
Well, they've got to be somewhere.
Did you check next door?

453
00:32:34,911 --> 00:32:37,330
At the Thompsons'?
They'd rather be in school!

454
00:32:45,630 --> 00:32:46,923
Nick, be careful!

455
00:32:47,966 --> 00:32:49,217
Yes, Mother.

456
00:32:50,969 --> 00:32:52,178
I'll go up with them.

457
00:33:01,353 --> 00:33:04,190
- There was a bee on me.
- Yeah, right.

458
00:33:08,444 --> 00:33:09,737
Wait a second.

459
00:33:11,780 --> 00:33:13,657
Nick doesn't play baseball.

460
00:33:18,829 --> 00:33:20,206
I just figured it out.

461
00:33:20,539 --> 00:33:23,000
I never woke up this morning.
This is all a bad dream.

462
00:33:23,459 --> 00:33:26,170
- Ron, get up.
- You're just a nightmare!

463
00:33:26,420 --> 00:33:28,422
When I get up,
Dad and I will be going fishing.

464
00:33:28,797 --> 00:33:30,174
I'm warning you.

465
00:33:34,136 --> 00:33:37,389
Okay, Ron... maybe you're right.

466
00:33:38,599 --> 00:33:40,309
Maybe this is just all a bad dream.

467
00:33:41,185 --> 00:33:42,978
But if it is...

468
00:33:44,021 --> 00:33:45,356
would this hurt?

469
00:33:45,439 --> 00:33:46,732
Get up!

470
00:33:48,234 --> 00:33:50,486
If you were my brother,
I'd put myself up for adoption.

471
00:33:50,569 --> 00:33:53,239
Yeah? I hope your face
ends up on a milk carton.

472
00:34:04,416 --> 00:34:05,960
My chair.

473
00:34:09,672 --> 00:34:11,048
My couch.

474
00:34:16,387 --> 00:34:17,596
It works.

475
00:34:26,272 --> 00:34:28,440
Nick? Amy?

476
00:34:29,400 --> 00:34:30,776
Can you hear me?

477
00:34:33,028 --> 00:34:34,113
Nick.

478
00:35:33,422 --> 00:35:34,923
Uh-huh.

479
00:35:47,895 --> 00:35:49,229
Nick?

480
00:35:50,439 --> 00:35:51,690
Nick!

481
00:35:56,153 --> 00:35:58,614
Nick!

482
00:35:59,656 --> 00:36:00,991
Are you okay?

483
00:36:04,328 --> 00:36:07,706
When we crashed, my entire life
flashed before my eyes.

484
00:36:09,500 --> 00:36:10,876
It didn't take too long.

485
00:36:15,130 --> 00:36:16,465
I'm scared, Russ.

486
00:36:17,383 --> 00:36:18,967
We could be anywhere now.

487
00:36:21,303 --> 00:36:22,763
I think we're still
in your yard.

488
00:36:23,180 --> 00:36:23,972
How do you know?

489
00:36:24,056 --> 00:36:25,891
Any other yard,
the grass would be shorter.

490
00:36:25,974 --> 00:36:28,018
Come on, we gotta find
Ron and your sister.

491
00:36:28,977 --> 00:36:31,647
And then if they don't fry him,
he'll go to jail. Your mother, too.

492
00:36:31,855 --> 00:36:33,190
After all, she's the one
who paid for it.

493
00:36:33,273 --> 00:36:35,734
That makes her an accomplice.
Do you know what it's like in jail?

494
00:36:35,818 --> 00:36:37,986
I am gonna tell you
one more time to shut up!

495
00:36:38,070 --> 00:36:41,323
And then what? And then you'll smack
me? You'll go to jail, too.

496
00:36:41,407 --> 00:36:43,492
I'll tell them
after a big bee ate my brother,

497
00:36:43,659 --> 00:36:44,827
you smacked me around.

498
00:36:45,369 --> 00:36:47,538
No jury in the world
would fail to convict you.

499
00:36:48,205 --> 00:36:49,998
The whole Szalinski family
is gonna be in jail for life!

500
00:36:50,082 --> 00:36:52,626
Look. My dad's machine works.

501
00:36:52,960 --> 00:36:55,003
When we get home
and he fixes us all,

502
00:36:55,087 --> 00:36:57,881
we're gonna be so rich,
you'll regret this.

503
00:37:00,467 --> 00:37:03,220
Amy, you know I've
always liked your family.

504
00:37:03,595 --> 00:37:05,180
I mean, your dad's a real nice guy.

505
00:37:05,514 --> 00:37:07,141
He's not as weird
as I thought he was.

506
00:37:07,516 --> 00:37:10,227
I love you and Nick
like my own brother and sister.

507
00:37:30,456 --> 00:37:33,000
It's just that my dad
doesn't understand your dad.

508
00:37:33,125 --> 00:37:35,002
Your dad doesn't understand anything.

509
00:38:16,961 --> 00:38:20,172
No, Cathy. I just thought
they might have stopped by.

510
00:38:20,256 --> 00:38:22,550
Okay, listen. If you see 'em,
just send 'em home?

511
00:38:23,050 --> 00:38:25,011
Okay. You have a good weekend.

512
00:38:27,722 --> 00:38:30,266
This is getting ridiculous.
Where the hell are those kids?

513
00:38:36,022 --> 00:38:37,440
No.

514
00:38:43,154 --> 00:38:44,071
We had to bring the dog.

515
00:38:44,155 --> 00:38:45,448
I don't wanna hear anymore
about the cat.

516
00:38:45,531 --> 00:38:47,658
It's the Forresters.
What are we gonna tell them?

517
00:38:47,742 --> 00:38:50,745
- How about the truth?
- Mae, what kind of an excuse is that?

518
00:38:51,746 --> 00:38:52,747
Don!

519
00:38:52,830 --> 00:38:54,874
Hey, what's up?

520
00:38:54,999 --> 00:38:56,917
How about catching some...

521
00:38:59,920 --> 00:39:01,964
Good morning, Russell.

522
00:39:02,048 --> 00:39:04,634
- Hi, Gloria.
- Say good morning, Sushi.

523
00:39:04,717 --> 00:39:06,135
- Don, I...
- Well, the fishing report

524
00:39:06,218 --> 00:39:09,180
looks stellar. Let's make exhaust
while the sun still shines, huh?

525
00:39:09,263 --> 00:39:11,265
- I called Charlie at Bass Lake.
- I know, but Don...

526
00:39:11,349 --> 00:39:12,850
He says the fish
are jumping on the boat.

527
00:39:12,933 --> 00:39:14,018
Oh! And I brought my new...

528
00:39:14,101 --> 00:39:16,729
Donald, that man over there
is flying.

529
00:39:17,188 --> 00:39:18,147
Right, Gloria.

530
00:39:18,314 --> 00:39:20,775
Don, uh, something's come up.
I don't think we're gonna be able

531
00:39:20,858 --> 00:39:22,652
to make it.
Maybe you ought to go on ahead.

532
00:39:23,069 --> 00:39:24,904
What do... What do you mean
you don't think you're gonna

533
00:39:24,987 --> 00:39:26,614
be able to make it?
We're caravaning, good buddy!

534
00:39:26,697 --> 00:39:29,158
- Donald, I am...
- Not now, Gloria!

535
00:39:29,241 --> 00:39:32,078
And besides, if you're not there
by six, you'll lose your deposit.

536
00:39:32,161 --> 00:39:33,245
Confidentially...

537
00:39:35,081 --> 00:39:36,624
Mae hasn't been feeling too well.

538
00:39:38,376 --> 00:39:39,418
Plumbing.

539
00:39:40,753 --> 00:39:41,879
Plumbing?

540
00:39:43,798 --> 00:39:46,550
Well, this isn't the big
Russ Thompson I know. I mean,

541
00:39:46,676 --> 00:39:49,512
Plumbing would not stop
the big Russ Thompson I know.

542
00:39:49,595 --> 00:39:51,722
Especially with
an $80 deposit on the line!

543
00:39:51,806 --> 00:39:54,600
Don, hook a big one for me, okay?

544
00:40:05,486 --> 00:40:06,654
Don?

545
00:40:11,367 --> 00:40:12,827
Let's roll, Gloria.

546
00:40:13,244 --> 00:40:14,787
- Come on, get in.
- What's wrong with...

547
00:40:15,037 --> 00:40:16,122
Come on.

548
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:21,836
Mm-hmm.

549
00:40:28,884 --> 00:40:30,761
- Did you tell him the truth?
- Honey!

550
00:40:32,012 --> 00:40:33,931
- Did they take it okay?
- Mae.

551
00:40:38,144 --> 00:40:41,439
80 bucks, Mae. Non-refundable!

552
00:40:41,522 --> 00:40:43,399
Those kids are grounded!

553
00:40:51,115 --> 00:40:52,199
Ugh...

554
00:40:52,283 --> 00:40:54,577
Mud is still mud,
no matter how small you are.

555
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:58,706
I can't believe some stores
actually charge for this stuff.

556
00:41:03,335 --> 00:41:05,504
That was really great,
what you did for Nick.

557
00:41:06,630 --> 00:41:09,300
What about you, Amy?
He saved you, too.

558
00:41:12,094 --> 00:41:13,637
Yeah, I know, Nick.

559
00:41:16,098 --> 00:41:18,100
Where'd you learn
artificial respiration?

560
00:41:21,020 --> 00:41:23,689
- French class, kid.
- Oh.

561
00:41:25,274 --> 00:41:26,567
French class?

562
00:41:46,253 --> 00:41:47,630
How about a truce, okay?

563
00:41:51,383 --> 00:41:52,510
Okay.

564
00:42:02,645 --> 00:42:04,855
I think this is the flagstone
in the middle of the yard.

565
00:42:05,898 --> 00:42:07,233
You mean we're halfway there?

566
00:42:07,566 --> 00:42:08,943
Yeah, I think so.

567
00:42:09,109 --> 00:42:10,361
Let's go!

568
00:42:17,117 --> 00:42:18,452
French class.

569
00:42:33,217 --> 00:42:35,553
I'd die for a strawberry sundae...

570
00:42:35,970 --> 00:42:38,639
with chocolate sprinkles
and a banana split.

571
00:42:40,599 --> 00:42:43,018
I could eat a corn dog
the size of a truck.

572
00:42:44,144 --> 00:42:47,273
Ron, if you had a corn dog,
it would be the size of a truck.

573
00:42:49,441 --> 00:42:51,151
I've died and gone to heaven.

574
00:42:51,443 --> 00:42:53,070
It's as big as a house!

575
00:42:53,487 --> 00:42:54,822
I saw it first! It's mine!

576
00:42:55,990 --> 00:42:58,117
I got dibs on the cream filling!

577
00:42:58,284 --> 00:42:59,952
Nick, it's one of your cookies!

578
00:43:09,879 --> 00:43:11,796
They're never gonna
believe this at school.

579
00:43:12,673 --> 00:43:13,883
You guys, come on.

580
00:43:14,717 --> 00:43:16,594
Somebody take this cookie!

581
00:43:17,511 --> 00:43:19,388
Nicky, take this thing!

582
00:43:20,514 --> 00:43:22,850
Help! Nicky!

583
00:43:24,476 --> 00:43:26,812
Come on, you guys.
This is your ant!

584
00:43:27,646 --> 00:43:29,273
- Nicky, help me!
- She did it.

585
00:43:30,232 --> 00:43:31,692
- Somebody help me!
- You know...

586
00:43:32,526 --> 00:43:34,612
- your sister's not bad...
- Nicky!

587
00:43:34,820 --> 00:43:36,655
- for a girl.
- Of course.

588
00:43:50,503 --> 00:43:53,172
Thank you, officer. We'd appreciate
you sending somebody by.

589
00:43:53,506 --> 00:43:55,508
Oh, okay, it's 646--

590
00:43:59,970 --> 00:44:02,306
That guy's got serious problems.

591
00:44:03,265 --> 00:44:05,101
<i>Mrs. Thompson. Hello?</i>

592
00:44:06,018 --> 00:44:08,938
646 Sycamore. Yes.

593
00:44:12,441 --> 00:44:14,026
Great idea, Russ,

594
00:44:14,193 --> 00:44:16,112
even if the boy genius
didn't think of it.

595
00:44:16,195 --> 00:44:17,822
Drawbridge!

596
00:44:21,283 --> 00:44:23,035
At this rate,
we'll be home by dinner.

597
00:44:24,203 --> 00:44:25,996
Maybe I can still go out...

598
00:44:27,581 --> 00:44:28,833
with Patty.

599
00:44:29,917 --> 00:44:33,462
Well, our weekend's shot.
We'll never get to go camping now.

600
00:44:33,546 --> 00:44:35,840
I know Dad.
We're grounded.

601
00:44:36,257 --> 00:44:38,217
We are camping, Ron. Only better.

602
00:44:38,884 --> 00:44:40,177
This is like a safari.

603
00:44:40,469 --> 00:44:43,639
Hey, yeah. I never thought
of it that way, you know?

604
00:44:44,181 --> 00:44:45,808
Onward!

605
00:45:02,700 --> 00:45:04,076
Amy?

606
00:45:05,119 --> 00:45:06,454
Nick?

607
00:45:07,997 --> 00:45:09,248
Wayne?

608
00:45:18,424 --> 00:45:22,052
Yeah. Um, I would like to report,
um, two missing children.

609
00:45:22,136 --> 00:45:24,597
Well, instead of flapping
your gums about it, why don't you go out--

610
00:45:24,680 --> 00:45:26,098
I'm sure
it's nothing to worry about.

611
00:45:26,182 --> 00:45:28,809
This sort of thing happens all the time.
You have a little spat

612
00:45:28,893 --> 00:45:30,394
- with the kids...
- Whoa, we did not have a spat.

613
00:45:30,519 --> 00:45:32,480
You were kind of upset
about Russ quitting the team, hon.

614
00:45:32,563 --> 00:45:34,106
Quit! I thought he was cut!

615
00:45:34,607 --> 00:45:36,442
He was afraid to tell you he quit.

616
00:45:36,525 --> 00:45:38,611
Quit... What do you mean
he was afraid to tell me?

617
00:45:38,694 --> 00:45:40,738
He can tell me anything he wants.
You know me! I always listen!

618
00:45:40,863 --> 00:45:42,114
Uh-huh.

619
00:45:42,990 --> 00:45:44,325
What's "uh-huh" supposed
to mean?

620
00:45:44,408 --> 00:45:45,951
- Russell!
- No. I don't like the way he said it.

621
00:45:46,035 --> 00:45:47,995
You don't need
to get upset, Mr. Thomson.

622
00:45:48,287 --> 00:45:50,039
You know, it's not unusual
for these runaways...

623
00:45:50,122 --> 00:45:53,083
Whoa, my kids
did not run away. They're happy kids,

624
00:45:53,167 --> 00:45:55,336
and there's no way you can make
me believe that they ran away!

625
00:45:56,504 --> 00:45:57,671
Here he goes with his writing again.

626
00:45:57,755 --> 00:45:59,965
- Don't you ever say ever anything?
- Uh-huh.

627
00:46:00,049 --> 00:46:01,717
Russell!

628
00:46:02,301 --> 00:46:04,887
Why do I feel like
I am the one on trial here?

629
00:46:05,012 --> 00:46:07,598
- I can't imagine, dear.
- What do you mean, you can't imagine?

630
00:46:07,681 --> 00:46:10,059
Well, I think we have enough
to get started.

631
00:46:10,267 --> 00:46:13,103
Please, give us a call
if you hear from the boys.

632
00:46:13,354 --> 00:46:14,438
Fine.

633
00:46:22,821 --> 00:46:24,657
Don't they need
a warrant or something?

634
00:46:24,823 --> 00:46:27,368
Russell, there's a few
things about yourself

635
00:46:27,451 --> 00:46:28,744
I think you should know.

636
00:46:29,578 --> 00:46:31,580
Well, like what?

637
00:46:31,830 --> 00:46:33,499
Well, I think you need
to sit down...

638
00:46:36,126 --> 00:46:38,504
Oh, another
missing children report.

639
00:46:39,088 --> 00:46:40,839
644 Sycamore.

640
00:46:42,132 --> 00:46:43,425
That's next door.

641
00:46:44,969 --> 00:46:47,096
I called the police.
What's on your head?

642
00:46:47,304 --> 00:46:49,640
- I was looking for the kids.
- Where, in a coal mine?

643
00:46:49,723 --> 00:46:51,892
- They're in the backyard.
- They are?

644
00:46:51,976 --> 00:46:54,061
Diane, I got something
real important to tell you.

645
00:46:55,020 --> 00:46:56,897
That is the couch from the attic.

646
00:46:58,816 --> 00:47:00,568
You can see the marks
where Quark chewed the arms.

647
00:47:00,651 --> 00:47:02,987
I found it on the floor.
It's my thinking couch.

648
00:47:04,280 --> 00:47:05,447
Wayne...

649
00:47:06,657 --> 00:47:07,992
are you trying to tell me...

650
00:47:09,660 --> 00:47:10,744
you did it?

651
00:47:11,620 --> 00:47:12,746
It works?

652
00:47:13,289 --> 00:47:14,623
The machine works?

653
00:47:15,249 --> 00:47:17,918
- Do the kids know?
- Well, yeah. The kids know.

654
00:47:18,252 --> 00:47:20,087
- That's great.
- It's not that great.

655
00:47:20,254 --> 00:47:21,839
- Why?
- I shrunk the kids.

656
00:47:22,715 --> 00:47:24,133
- What?
- And the Thompson kids, too.

657
00:47:24,216 --> 00:47:25,843
They're about this big.
They're in the backyard.

658
00:47:26,135 --> 00:47:28,053
- What?
- I threw them out with the trash.

659
00:47:28,262 --> 00:47:30,681
Stay here. I'll get the door.

660
00:47:32,683 --> 00:47:33,767
Yes?

661
00:47:34,643 --> 00:47:37,938
Did, uh, you report
some missing children?

662
00:47:38,647 --> 00:47:40,941
Oh, there must be some mistake.
Ours are in the backyard.

663
00:47:41,233 --> 00:47:42,401
Right, honey?

664
00:47:45,696 --> 00:47:46,905
She's...

665
00:47:47,865 --> 00:47:49,158
Thank you!

666
00:48:03,756 --> 00:48:06,800
How are you doing, boy?
How about a pit stop?

667
00:48:13,140 --> 00:48:15,726
- What is it, Ron?
- Antie needs a recharge.

668
00:48:22,608 --> 00:48:23,901
Here, you deserve this.

669
00:48:25,986 --> 00:48:28,072
We're not gonna make it before dark.

670
00:48:29,114 --> 00:48:30,908
Yeah, even if we did,

671
00:48:30,991 --> 00:48:32,368
the mall would be closed.

672
00:48:33,035 --> 00:48:34,536
Don't eat so fast.

673
00:48:34,953 --> 00:48:36,997
Hey... that's my hand.

674
00:48:40,125 --> 00:48:41,502
Why don't we let him go?

675
00:48:41,627 --> 00:48:43,754
He's worn out, and his family
might be worried about him.

676
00:48:44,672 --> 00:48:47,591
- It's okay with me.
- Yeah, me too, I guess.

677
00:48:47,758 --> 00:48:49,218
Help me get off the harness.

678
00:48:59,269 --> 00:49:00,521
You can go now.

679
00:49:05,317 --> 00:49:07,736
Come on. Just start walking.
He'll get the idea.

680
00:49:10,948 --> 00:49:12,408
Thanks for the lift, Antie.

681
00:49:13,283 --> 00:49:15,494
You're free.
You get home now.

682
00:49:16,120 --> 00:49:18,163
Shoo!

683
00:49:20,624 --> 00:49:22,126
Looks like you've got a new pet.

684
00:49:22,584 --> 00:49:24,420
Dumb ant doesn't know
what's best for him.

685
00:49:26,380 --> 00:49:28,382
Go on! Go home!

686
00:49:28,549 --> 00:49:30,134
Mom will never let me keep you.

687
00:49:37,558 --> 00:49:38,684
Amy?

688
00:49:39,059 --> 00:49:40,269
- Nick?
- Nick?

689
00:49:40,477 --> 00:49:42,438
- Are you there, kids?
- Nicky!

690
00:49:43,439 --> 00:49:44,857
Where are you?

691
00:49:47,151 --> 00:49:49,111
- Any luck?
- No.

692
00:50:05,711 --> 00:50:06,795
Nick?

693
00:50:08,422 --> 00:50:09,465
Amy?

694
00:50:13,677 --> 00:50:15,012
Do you see anything yet?

695
00:50:15,929 --> 00:50:17,306
It's too thick.

696
00:50:18,390 --> 00:50:20,058
It's supposed to be thick.
It's grass!

697
00:50:21,894 --> 00:50:22,853
Keep looking.

698
00:50:23,395 --> 00:50:25,230
- Nick?
- Amy?

699
00:50:31,737 --> 00:50:33,906
- What was that?
- Ow!

700
00:50:34,448 --> 00:50:36,492
Wa... Watch out!

701
00:50:38,494 --> 00:50:39,703
Come on.

702
00:50:49,171 --> 00:50:51,423
- Ugh...
- Where did that come from?

703
00:50:58,680 --> 00:51:01,225
- Yeah, it's Dad's brand.
- He told us he quit!

704
00:51:05,020 --> 00:51:07,272
Ugh. Looks like he started up again.

705
00:51:08,315 --> 00:51:10,984
No, he only smokes when
he's really worried about something.

706
00:51:13,278 --> 00:51:16,615
Nicky, be careful!

707
00:51:18,575 --> 00:51:20,869
Hey. Hey, maybe we can use this.

708
00:51:36,927 --> 00:51:39,513
Cool! Now, we can see in the dark!

709
00:51:45,602 --> 00:51:48,313
Hey, Antie, look! Headlights!

710
00:51:49,481 --> 00:51:51,024
I didn't force him
to join the team.

711
00:51:51,108 --> 00:51:54,486
I know, but you always
make such a big deal out of sports!

712
00:51:54,653 --> 00:51:55,779
Because sports build character.

713
00:51:55,863 --> 00:51:57,614
No, they don't.
They build muscle!

714
00:51:58,532 --> 00:52:00,158
Russell, you've got to understand...

715
00:52:01,118 --> 00:52:02,035
Wayne?

716
00:52:02,119 --> 00:52:04,162
Yeah?

717
00:52:04,997 --> 00:52:06,582
We've got to tell the Thompsons.

718
00:52:17,759 --> 00:52:20,012
What do you take me for?
A complete idiot?

719
00:52:21,179 --> 00:52:23,181
- How did this happen?
- Hold on a second, Mae.

720
00:52:23,515 --> 00:52:24,683
I'll handle it, all right?

721
00:52:25,726 --> 00:52:26,685
How did this happen?

722
00:52:26,768 --> 00:52:28,604
Well, the machine analyzes
the molecular structure

723
00:52:28,687 --> 00:52:31,148
of each compound, and then
through laser inversion, it takes...

724
00:52:31,231 --> 00:52:34,484
Wait, Wayne, wait. I think
the Thompsons need to see the couch.

725
00:52:36,695 --> 00:52:38,864
This guy is a waste of skin.

726
00:52:40,824 --> 00:52:42,868
- This is a microscope.
- I know.

727
00:52:44,786 --> 00:52:45,996
Look in there.

728
00:52:47,247 --> 00:52:48,373
All right.

729
00:52:49,708 --> 00:52:52,169
You see, that's the couch that used
to be sitting right over here

730
00:52:52,252 --> 00:52:53,462
until the machine shrunk it.

731
00:52:53,545 --> 00:52:55,172
All right,
so there's some doll furniture.

732
00:52:55,297 --> 00:52:57,674
Big deal. They sell
that kind of stuff at swap meets!

733
00:52:58,884 --> 00:53:00,385
Why didn't you tell us earlier?

734
00:53:00,928 --> 00:53:03,889
Well, until now,
the machine just blew things up.

735
00:53:04,097 --> 00:53:05,974
- Are you saying that machine blew...
- Blew up my kids?

736
00:53:06,058 --> 00:53:07,434
- No, no, no.
- No, no, no.

737
00:53:07,517 --> 00:53:10,270
If the machine had blown up the kids,
there'd be pieces of them everywhere.

738
00:53:10,354 --> 00:53:11,438
Wayne!

739
00:53:12,314 --> 00:53:13,398
Sorry, I...

740
00:53:13,774 --> 00:53:16,318
Look, I'm positive about this, okay?

741
00:53:16,693 --> 00:53:18,028
The machine shrunk our kids.

742
00:53:18,195 --> 00:53:19,696
You're the one who needs
a shrink, Szalinski!

743
00:53:19,780 --> 00:53:21,698
- Russell!
- You are a nutcase.

744
00:53:21,782 --> 00:53:25,535
And I'll tell you something.
I have got an air hammer in my attic.

745
00:53:25,702 --> 00:53:27,245
And if you did do something
to my kids,

746
00:53:27,329 --> 00:53:29,164
there's gonna be pieces of you
all over the neighborhood!

747
00:53:29,331 --> 00:53:30,415
Come on, honey.

748
00:53:36,838 --> 00:53:39,341
I think that went well.

749
00:53:40,300 --> 00:53:42,135
I think we should have them
over more often.

750
00:53:43,011 --> 00:53:44,888
Yeah, well,
it's a priority for me.

751
00:53:45,472 --> 00:53:47,182
Now why don't you do
what you're paid to do?

752
00:53:49,142 --> 00:53:50,936
Find my kids.

753
00:54:03,281 --> 00:54:04,574
I thought you didn't believe him.

754
00:54:06,410 --> 00:54:07,577
Well, I don't.

755
00:54:09,579 --> 00:54:10,747
Electricity's cheap.

756
00:54:35,439 --> 00:54:36,648
Come on!

757
00:54:37,024 --> 00:54:39,818
Look at this!
We don't have to sleep on the ground.

758
00:54:43,613 --> 00:54:45,782
- What is it?
- One of my legos.

759
00:54:46,908 --> 00:54:48,201
Give me a boost!

760
00:54:52,748 --> 00:54:54,041
Hey...

761
00:54:55,542 --> 00:54:56,710
not bad.

762
00:54:58,962 --> 00:54:59,963
Yeah.

763
00:55:00,047 --> 00:55:01,840
I don't even have to brush my teeth!

764
00:55:03,550 --> 00:55:04,593
Quit pushing!

765
00:55:04,885 --> 00:55:07,262
- Move over!
- You move over!

766
00:55:08,388 --> 00:55:10,599
- Why don't you take that one?
- Are you sure?

767
00:55:11,558 --> 00:55:13,977
Yeah. I'd rather sleep down here.

768
00:55:20,692 --> 00:55:21,735
Night, Antie.

769
00:55:26,490 --> 00:55:27,616
Think they'll be warm enough?

770
00:55:28,700 --> 00:55:30,160
They look fine to me.

771
00:55:42,881 --> 00:55:43,923
You think you'll be warm enough?

772
00:55:43,923 --> 00:55:44,424
I'm sorry.

773
00:55:46,176 --> 00:55:47,761
That's not what I meant.

774
00:55:49,221 --> 00:55:51,848
You know,
it's not important...

775
00:55:52,724 --> 00:55:54,351
if I sell another house,

776
00:55:55,477 --> 00:55:57,938
or if you get a grant
this year or next.

777
00:56:00,148 --> 00:56:02,526
We've just got to get
this family back together.

778
00:56:05,987 --> 00:56:07,614
I feel the same way.

779
00:56:17,541 --> 00:56:20,126
I gotta fix the machine.
You should get some sleep.

780
00:56:20,794 --> 00:56:23,129
We gotta get up early and come
out here and look for them.

781
00:56:24,589 --> 00:56:25,966
No, I couldn't sleep.

782
00:56:27,801 --> 00:56:29,678
Not with my babies
out here all alone.

783
00:56:30,929 --> 00:56:33,515
Ah, don't worry about them. They got
the Thompson kids with them.

784
00:56:33,932 --> 00:56:35,141
They'll be fine.

785
00:56:36,935 --> 00:56:38,770
That's another thing
I'm worried about.

786
00:56:39,688 --> 00:56:41,982
Amy in the dark...

787
00:56:42,566 --> 00:56:44,025
with little Russ Thompson.

788
00:56:44,568 --> 00:56:45,861
Get some rest.

789
00:56:52,409 --> 00:56:54,286
They'd better behave themselves.

790
00:56:59,332 --> 00:57:00,667
It's funny.

791
00:57:01,543 --> 00:57:04,129
The moon looks the same size
whether you're big or small.

792
00:57:04,588 --> 00:57:07,132
Yeah. Tell that to my dad.

793
00:57:09,175 --> 00:57:11,136
Why? Does he pick on you
about your size?

794
00:57:12,679 --> 00:57:14,431
The bigger the guy,
the bigger the moon.

795
00:57:16,016 --> 00:57:17,517
It's just the way he thinks.

796
00:57:18,685 --> 00:57:19,644
Russ?

797
00:57:21,396 --> 00:57:23,648
- Yeah?
- How come you never came over before?

798
00:57:25,901 --> 00:57:26,985
Well...

799
00:57:31,072 --> 00:57:32,157
I mean...

800
00:57:32,866 --> 00:57:35,035
you talked to me
once or twice last year.

801
00:57:36,411 --> 00:57:39,331
I mean, I would've come by.
I wanted to, but...

802
00:57:41,833 --> 00:57:43,835
I guess I always thought
you were too popular to notice me.

803
00:57:45,503 --> 00:57:47,380
I was too popular to notice.

804
00:57:49,424 --> 00:57:50,634
I was stupid.

805
00:58:19,079 --> 00:58:21,623
Nicky!

806
00:58:23,124 --> 00:58:23,208
Look, it's the rest of them.

807
00:58:26,294 --> 00:58:27,921
I'm gonna go get the Thompsons.

808
00:58:28,171 --> 00:58:29,256
Hang on, kids!

809
00:58:31,466 --> 00:58:33,885
It obviously worked on the kids.
I just don't get it.

810
00:58:35,679 --> 00:58:37,431
Well, you better get it, Szalinski.

811
00:58:37,514 --> 00:58:39,391
Russ, you're not helping.

812
00:58:39,474 --> 00:58:42,102
- So, I'm not helping.
- Russell, you got to understand.

813
00:58:43,186 --> 00:58:44,688
Baseball!

814
00:58:44,771 --> 00:58:46,648
Nick's trying to say something.

815
00:58:47,190 --> 00:58:48,692
I can't make it out.

816
00:58:51,445 --> 00:58:53,530
The baseball, Dad!

817
00:58:54,197 --> 00:58:55,991
What are you saying, Nick?

818
00:59:00,287 --> 00:59:01,663
Swinging?

819
00:59:01,913 --> 00:59:04,624
- Sounds like--
- It's semaphore, Mae.

820
00:59:04,708 --> 00:59:07,127
Wait! Uh...uh, flapping!

821
00:59:07,586 --> 00:59:08,879
- Pitching.
- Um...

822
00:59:09,129 --> 00:59:11,089
- Throwing.
- You're out! You're out!

823
00:59:11,631 --> 00:59:12,758
Baseball!

824
00:59:13,049 --> 00:59:14,134
- Yes!
- Right!

825
00:59:14,259 --> 00:59:15,802
- The baseball!
- The ball!

826
00:59:16,052 --> 00:59:17,262
Baseball?

827
00:59:17,846 --> 00:59:19,222
Good, honey.

828
00:59:22,100 --> 00:59:24,478
Holy smokes. It's the laser!

829
00:59:26,438 --> 00:59:28,565
I got it.
The ball came through the window,

830
00:59:28,940 --> 00:59:30,358
and activated the machine,

831
00:59:31,193 --> 00:59:32,861
and got in the path of the laser.

832
00:59:33,487 --> 00:59:34,905
Why didn't I think of this before?

833
00:59:35,363 --> 00:59:37,199
- I could take a wild guess.
- Shh!

834
00:59:37,282 --> 00:59:39,409
It's creating too much heat
That's way everything was blowing up!

835
00:59:39,701 --> 00:59:42,204
- Thanks, Nick!
- All right, Nicky!

836
00:59:42,287 --> 00:59:43,663
Diane, you take the spoon.
I'll get the machine...

837
00:59:43,747 --> 00:59:46,291
Hold it, Szalinski.
This gizmo's been blowing up fruit.

838
00:59:46,374 --> 00:59:48,877
And you're not trying it on my kids
until you try is on something living!

839
00:59:53,173 --> 00:59:55,008
You know something?
Maybe I should do it on myself.

840
00:59:55,091 --> 00:59:56,885
I'll show you how to
work the machine. It's not that hard.

841
00:59:56,968 --> 00:59:58,887
No, no!

842
00:59:59,095 --> 01:00:02,057
- Do it on me.
- Are you sure, honey?

843
01:00:07,521 --> 01:00:08,939
How'd those get there?

844
01:00:24,871 --> 01:00:26,039
Okay, Szalinski,

845
01:00:26,832 --> 01:00:27,749
shoot.

846
01:00:28,542 --> 01:00:30,377
- No reason this shouldn't work now.
- Huh?

847
01:00:45,475 --> 01:00:46,768
Stand back!

848
01:00:46,893 --> 01:00:50,981
- Honey, you're so brave.
- Come on, Mae. Come on. Come.

849
01:01:04,744 --> 01:01:07,747
- Russ, are you okay?
- Yeah, why?

850
01:01:23,680 --> 01:01:24,806
So...

851
01:01:25,724 --> 01:01:26,975
if this works,

852
01:01:27,058 --> 01:01:28,643
do you want to go
to the dance on Friday?

853
01:01:30,061 --> 01:01:31,354
Can you dance?

854
01:01:33,690 --> 01:01:36,401
- Not really.
- I'd love to.

855
01:01:56,504 --> 01:01:57,339
Oh!

856
01:01:57,422 --> 01:01:58,965
- Russ, Ron!
- Dad!

857
01:01:59,049 --> 01:02:02,344
Oh, I knew my boys wouldn't run away.

858
01:02:03,637 --> 01:02:05,972
- Are you and Dad okay?
- Yeah.

859
01:02:06,056 --> 01:02:06,389
- All the way across the yard....
- We're great.

860
01:02:06,514 --> 01:02:09,643
I wanted to go fishing, Dad.
I didn't mean to mess everything up.

861
01:02:09,726 --> 01:02:11,937
- I know, Ronnie. I know.
- Dad?

862
01:02:12,729 --> 01:02:14,105
If you really want me
back on the team...

863
01:02:14,189 --> 01:02:16,441
I don't care about the team.
I care about you!

864
01:02:17,150 --> 01:02:18,652
I'm proud of you.

865
01:02:20,403 --> 01:02:21,529
I need to take a shower!

866
01:02:21,696 --> 01:02:22,948
Szalinski.

867
01:02:32,457 --> 01:02:33,625
Let's eat!

868
01:02:33,708 --> 01:02:35,168
- Honey, make a toast.
- Good idea.

869
01:02:35,251 --> 01:02:36,544
- Oh, great.
- Oh, I love toasts.

870
01:02:36,628 --> 01:02:38,505
To the Thompsons and the Szalinskis,

871
01:02:38,588 --> 01:02:40,090
- and many more dinners together.
- Cheers.

872
01:02:40,173 --> 01:02:41,216
Though I think

873
01:02:41,299 --> 01:02:42,926
we're gonna be having leftovers
for a while.

874
01:02:43,551 --> 01:02:45,512
Wonder what's for dinner.
Right, Russ?

875
01:02:45,845 --> 01:02:47,263
Well, I guess I gotta carve
this thing, huh?

876
01:02:47,347 --> 01:02:49,015
Hey, want to use
my chainsaw?

877
01:02:49,099 --> 01:02:50,684
Who wants bread?

878
01:03:02,445 --> 01:03:03,780
Hey, wait!

879
01:03:04,322 --> 01:03:05,615
I get it!

880
01:03:06,408 --> 01:03:09,202
French class! Ha-ha!
